<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kakegawa Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kakegawa Castle is a Japanese castle in Kakegawa, Shizuoka Prefecture, in the former province of Tōtōmi. Built in the early 16th century by a retainer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan">Imagawa clan</a>, it was where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-ujizane">Imagawa Ujizane</a> held out against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> before surrendering in 1569. Its keep, wrecked by an earthquake in 1854, was rebuilt in wood in 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kakegawa-castle.jpg" alt="Kakegawa Castle" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ShizuokaSensei at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kakegawacastleapril2006.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kakegawa Castle is a hirayama-style castle, raised on a small hill in the centre of Kakegawa. The town had been an important post station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tōkaidō</a> highway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> since the Heian period, and its position made it a strategic point for holding the eastern half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totomi-province">Tōtōmi Province</a>. The first castle was built by Asahina Yasuhiro, a retainer of the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshitada">Imagawa Yoshitada</a>, and it stayed with later generations of the Asahina clan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan">Imagawa clan</a>'s defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-okehazama">Battle of Okehazama</a>, its former lands were divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> of Kai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> of Mikawa. The castle passed to the Tokugawa after a siege of five months. The surrounding district was contested between the Tokugawa and the Takeda for many years, but the castle stayed in Tokugawa hands until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-clan">Takeda clan</a> fell.</p><p>After the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> in 1590, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> give up his domains in the Tōkai region for the Kantō. Kakegawa went to Hideyoshi's retainer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamauchi-kazutoyo">Yamauchi Kazutoyo</a> as the centre of a new domain of 51,000 koku, later 59,000. He rebuilt the castle completely to the latest designs, and its present layout and much of its stone walls and moats date from his time. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> the Yamauchi were moved to Kōchi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, and Kakegawa Domain passed first to Hisamatsu Sadakatsu, then to a series of fudai daimyō clans, ending with seven generations of the Ōta clan.</p><p>The Yamauchi keep was destroyed by an earthquake in 1604 and rebuilt in 1621. The Ansei Tōkai earthquake of 1854 did extensive damage. Many buildings were rebuilt by 1861 and served as local government offices after the Meiji Restoration, but the keep was left in ruins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Ni-no-Maru Goten, the daimyō's mansion built by Ōta Sukekatsu after the earthquake, survives and was registered as an Important Cultural Property in 1980, as do parts of the moats and walls, a guardhouse and the drum house. A gate of the main bailey built in 1659 is now the main gate of the temple of Yusan-ji in Fukuroi. In April 1994 the keep and parts of the innermost bailey were rebuilt by the original methods from a few surviving diagrams, the first keep in post-war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> reconstructed in wood in this way, at a cost of 1 billion yen raised largely through public donation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kakegawa Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Ujizane lost Sunpu to Takeda Shingen in 1568 and surrendered <b>Kakegawa</b> castle to Tokugawa Ieyasu in 1569, ending the domain.</blockquote><a href="/state/imagawa-clan-domain">Imagawa clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
